Richardson extrapolation starts with a $k$-sequence

\begin{equation}
\label{sequence-large-order}
{\mathbb S} (k) \simeq s_0 + \frac{s_1}{k} + \frac{s_2}{k^2} + \cdots,
\end{equation}

\noindent
from where its $N^{\text{th}}$-Richardson transform, herein denoted by $\mathrm{RT}_{{\mathbb S}} (0,k,N)$, is a new $k$-sequence which can be recursively defined as\footnote{A closed form expression for the $N^{\text{th}}$-Richardson transform is

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{RT}_{{\mathbb S}} (0,k,N) = \sum_{n=0}^N (-1)^{n+N}\, \frac{\left(k+n\right)^N}{n! \left(N-n\right)!}\, {\mathbb S}(k+n).
\end{equation}
}

\begin{eqnarray}
\mathrm{RT}_{{\mathbb S}} (0,k,0) &=& {\mathbb S}(k), \\
\mathrm{RT}_{{\mathbb S}} (0,k,N) &=& \mathrm{RT}_{{\mathbb S}} (0,k+1,N-1) + \frac{k}{N}\, \Big(\, \mathrm{RT}_{{\mathbb S}} (0,k+1,N-1) - \mathrm{RT}_{{\mathbb S}} (0,k,N-1)\, \Big), \\
&&
\hspace{+340pt} N \ge 1. \nonumber
