Let us begin with a $d$-dimensional ``partition function'', given by the integral

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:nd_integral}
Z (\hbar) = \frac{1}{\left( 2\pi \right)^d} \int_{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}} {\mathrm{d}} z^1 \wedge \cdots \wedge {\mathrm{d}} z^d\, {\mathrm{e}}^{- \frac{1}{\hbar} V(\boldsymbol{z})}.
\end{equation}

To be more more precise, let us introduce the set

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:chi}
{\cal X}_{\epsilon} = \left\{ \boldsymbol{z} \in {\mathbb C}^d\, \left|\, {\mathbb{R}}{\mathrm{e}} \left( - \frac{1}{\hbar} V (\boldsymbol{z}) \right) = - \frac{1}{\epsilon} \right. \right\},
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:decompositionI}
\int_{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}} {\mathrm{d}} z^1 \wedge \cdots \wedge {\mathrm{d}} z^d = \sum_{i=1}^N n_i\, \int_{{\cal J}_i} {\mathrm{d}} z^1 \wedge \cdots \wedge {\mathrm{d}} z^d,
\end{equation}

In order to construct the thimbles, start by labeling the critical points of the potential $V$ as\footnote{In order to avoid notational confusion, we shall denote the critical points with a subscript $\{ i,j,... \}$, and the coordinate vector index, whenever present, with a superscript $\{ a,b,... \}$.} $\boldsymbol{z}^*_i$ with $i \in \{ 1, 2, \dots, N \}$; satisfying

\begin{equation}
\left. \frac{\partial V}{\partial z^a} \right|_{\boldsymbol{z} = \boldsymbol{z}^*_i} = 0.
\end{equation}

Moreover, this convergence will be fastest along that collection of curves (parameterized by $\tau$) which flow along the \textit{gradient} of the above function, \textit{i.e.}, along

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gradient_flow}
\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\boldsymbol{z}}{{\mathrm{d}}\tau} = - 2\, \frac{\partial\rho}{\partial\bar{\boldsymbol{z}}}.
\end{equation}

It is then straightforward to show that $\rho \left( \boldsymbol{z}(\tau) \right)$ \textit{decreases} along any curve with increasing $\tau$:

\begin{equation}
\frac{{\mathrm{d}} \rho \left( \boldsymbol{z}(\tau) \right)}{{\mathrm{d}}\tau} = \frac{\partial\rho}{\partial\boldsymbol{z}} \cdot \frac{{\mathrm{d}}\boldsymbol{z}}{{\mathrm{d}}\tau} = - 2 \left| \frac{\partial\rho}{\partial\boldsymbol{z}} \right|^2 \leq 0.
\end{equation}

To see how this comes about, let us split the complex coordinates $\boldsymbol{z}$ into their real and imaginary parts, $\boldsymbol{z} = \boldsymbol{x} + {\mathrm{i}} \boldsymbol{y}$, and use the Cauchy--Riemann equations to obtain

\begin{eqnarray}
\label{eq:hamilton_flow-a}
\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\boldsymbol{x}}{{\mathrm{d}}\tau} &=& - \frac{\partial\rho}{\partial\boldsymbol{x}} = - \frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{y}}\, {\mathbb{I}}{\mathrm{m}} \left( - \frac{1}{\hbar} V (\boldsymbol{z}) \right), \\
\label{eq:hamilton_flow-b}
\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\boldsymbol{y}}{{\mathrm{d}}\tau} &=& - \frac{\partial\rho}{\partial\boldsymbol{y}} = \frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{x}}\, {\mathbb{I}}{\mathrm{m}} \left( - \frac{1}{\hbar} V (\boldsymbol{z}) \right),
\end{eqnarray}

One can combine the gradient flow equations for the real part, and the Hamiltonian flow equations for the imaginary part, into a single equation in complex variables (where we also make explicit the phase of $\hbar$, as in $\hbar = |\hbar|\, {\mathrm{e}}^{{\mathrm{i}}\theta}$)

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gradient_flow_complex}
\frac{{\mathrm{d}} z^a}{{\mathrm{d}}\tau} = \frac{{\mathrm{e}}^{{\mathrm{i}}\theta}}{|\hbar|}\, \frac{\partial\bar{V}}{\partial\bar{z}^a},
\end{equation}

define \textit{upward flow} as originating from a critical point and defined by the equations

\begin{equation}
\frac{{\mathrm{d}} z^a}{{\mathrm{d}}\tau} = - \frac{{\mathrm{e}}^{{\mathrm{i}}\theta}}{|\hbar|}\, \frac{\partial\bar{V}}{\partial\bar{z}^a}.

The set of all these cycles (upon adequate equivalence classes) constitute a basis for a dual homology class $H_d ( {\mathbb C}^d, {\cal Y}_\epsilon; {\mathbb Z} )$, where

\begin{equation}
{\cal Y}_{\epsilon} = \left\{ \boldsymbol{z} \in {\mathbb C}^d\, \left|\, {\mathbb{R}}{\mathrm{e}} \left( - \frac{1}{\hbar} V (\boldsymbol{z}) \right) = + \frac{1}{\epsilon} \right. \right\}. 
\end{equation}

